{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012H6bLBoroCW6TTMxacGQT8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"HB EC-HTE Synthetic Validation\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet-prior confusion matrix estimator with partial pooling. Full regime sweep: extreme/moderate/homogeneous × n_expert={100,250,500,1000,2000} × K={2,4}. 3600 MC runs total. MCMC diagnostics: PyMC NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"mcmc_rhat_max\": 1.004,\n7\t      \"mcmc_ess_min\": 1618,\n8\t      \"mcmc_divergent\": 0,\n9\t      \"total_mc_runs\": 3600,\n10\t      \"runtime_min\": 69,\n11\t      \"hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k2_n50\": \"4-9%\",\n12\t      \"hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k4_n50_moderate\": \"6-9%\",\n13\t      \"hb_coverage_median\": 0.93,\n14\t      \"hb_coverage_min\": 0.87,\n15\t      \"hyperprior_sensitivity_k2_variation\": \"<1%\",\n16\t      \"hyperprior_sensitivity_k4_variation\": \"~4-5%\",\n17\t      \"singularity_guard_triggers\": \"extreme/n=50 only\"\n18\t    },\n19\t    \"tables\": [\n20\t      {\n21\t        \"name\": \"Table A1: MCMC Diagnostics Summary\",\n22\t        \"columns\": [\n23\t          \"Config\",\n24\t          \"R-hat (max)\",\n25\t          \"ESS (min)\",\n26\t          \"Divergences\",\n27\t          \"Runtime (s)\"\n28\t        ],\n29\t        \"rows\": [\n30\t          [\n31\t            \"K=2/extreme/n=100\",\n32\t            \"1.003\",\n33\t            \"1842\",\n34\t            \"0\",\n35\t            \"68\"\n36\t          ],\n37\t          [\n38\t            \"K=2/moderate/n=100\",\n39\t            \"1.002\",\n40\t            \"2105\",\n41\t            \"0\",\n42\t            \"65\"\n43\t          ],\n44\t          [\n45\t            \"K=4/extreme/n=100\",\n46\t            \"1.004\",\n47\t            \"1618\",\n48\t            \"0\",\n49\t            \"82\"\n50\t          ],\n51\t          [\n52\t            \"K=4/moderate/n=100\",\n53\t            \"1.003\",\n54\t            \"1790\",\n55\t            \"0\",\n56\t            \"79\"\n57\t          ],\n58\t          [\n59\t            \"K=4/extreme/n=50 (guard)\",\n60\t            \"1.003\",\n61\t            \"1650\",\n62\t            \"0\",\n63\t            \"85\"\n64\t          ]\n65\t        ]\n66\t      },\n67\t      {\n68\t        \"name\": \"Table A2: HB vs MLE RMSE Comparison\",\n69\t        \"columns\": [\n70\t          \"Config\",\n71\t          \"HB RMSE\",\n72\t          \"MLE RMSE\",\n73\t          \"Improvement (%)\",\n74\t          \"HB Coverage\"\n75\t        ],\n76\t        \"rows\": [\n77\t          [\n78\t            \"K=2/extreme/n=50\",\n79\t            \"0.228\",\n80\t            \"0.248\",\n81\t            \"+8.1%\",\n82\t            \"0.91\"\n83\t          ],\n84\t          [\n85\t            \"K=2/extreme/n=100\",\n86\t            \"0.142\",\n87\t            \"0.145\",\n88\t            \"+2.1%\",\n89\t            \"0.93\"\n90\t          ],\n91\t          [\n92\t            \"K=2/extreme/n=250\",\n93\t            \"0.091\",\n94\t            \"0.093\",\n95\t            \"+2.2%\",\n96\t            \"0.94\"\n97\t          ],\n98\t          [\n99\t            \"K=4/moderate/n=50\",\n100\t            \"0.195\",\n101\t            \"0.213\",\n102\t            \"+8.5%\",\n103\t            \"0.89\"\n104\t          ],\n105\t          [\n106\t            \"K=4/extreme/n=50\",\n107\t            \"0.310\",\n108\t            \"0.382\",\n109\t            \"+18.8%\",\n110\t            \"0.87\"\n111\t          ],\n112\t          [\n113\t            \"K=4/extreme/n=100\",\n114\t            \"0.210\",\n115\t            \"0.225\",\n116\t            \"+6.7%\",\n117\t            \"0.92\"\n118\t          ]\n119\t        ]\n120\t      },\n121\t      {\n122\t        \"name\": \"Table A3: Hyperprior Sensitivity (Gamma scale)\",\n123\t        \"columns\": [\n124\t          \"Scale\",\n125\t          \"K=2 RMSE\",\n126\t          \"K=4 RMSE\",\n127\t          \"Coverage\"\n128\t        ],\n129\t        \"rows\": [\n130\t          [\n131\t            \"0.5x\",\n132\t            \"0.091\",\n133\t            \"0.208\",\n134\t            \"0.93\"\n135\t          ],\n136\t          [\n137\t            \"1.0x (base)\",\n138\t            \"0.091\",\n139\t            \"0.210\",\n140\t            \"0.93\"\n141\t          ],\n142\t          [\n143\t            \"2.0x\",\n144\t            \"0.091\",\n145\t            \"0.219\",\n146\t            \"0.93\"\n147\t          ]\n148\t        ]\n149\t      }\n150\t    ],\n151\t    \"figures\": [],\n152\t    \"analysis\": \"Reviewers in best tier see this as thorough validation infrastructure. The 4-chain MCMC fix (resolving initial K=4/extreme/n=50 blowup) is disclosed as methodological due diligence. Key narrative: HB provides meaningful but modest improvement over MLE — the contribution is NOT 'HB is dramatically better' but rather 'stratified correction with principled shrinkage enables reliable per-subgroup CM estimation.' This framing aligns with the paper's positioning as problem-identification rather than method-innovation. Reviewer concern about computational cost (69 min for full sweep) is preempted by noting this is a one-time calibration, not per-prediction cost.\\n\\n\\nBest tier assumes reviewers accept HB partial pooling as well-motivated James-Stein shrinkage. R̂<1.01 and ESS>200 (actual: ESS>1618 with 4-chain fix) satisfy standard MCMC convergence criteria — NLP reviewers without deep Bayesian background will see these as reassuring checkboxes. 4-9% RMSE improvement over MLE at small n is modest but directionally correct; reviewers accept this as 'partial pooling helps when data is scarce' (classical result, applied to new setting). Coverage median 0.93 (below nominal 0.95) is disclosed honestly — reviewer sees this as boundary condition acknowledgment, not flaw. Hyperprior sensitivity <5% for K=4 confirms robustness without requiring exhaustive sensitivity analysis — one table in appendix suffices.\\n\"\n153\t  },\n154\t  {\n155\t    \"name\": \"Bias Identity Verification\",\n156\t    \"description\": \"Formal derivation and empirical verification of bias = f(Δπ, Δτ) identity. Systematic parameter grid: Δπ ∈ {0, 0.05, ..., 0.25} × Δτ ∈ {0, 0.1, ..., 0.5} × K ∈ {2,4}. 'Debiasing can hurt' proposition with constructive two-subgroup example. 520 non-trivial configs.\\n\",\n157\t    \"results\": {\n158\t      \"r2_k2\": 0.994,\n159\t      \"r2_k4\": 0.974,\n160\t      \"tightness_ratio_k2_median\": 1.003,\n161\t      \"tightness_ratio_k4_median\": 1.023,\n162\t      \"debiasing_hurts_pct\": 50,\n163\t      \"debiasing_hurts_pct_detail\": \"260/520 configs (excluding Δτ=0)\",\n164\t      \"max_bias_ratio_k2\": 2.86,\n165\t      \"max_bias_ratio_k4\": 2.41,\n166\t      \"coverage_2se_k2\": 0.951,\n167\t      \"coverage_2se_k4\": 0.94,\n168\t      \"headline_median_amplification\": \"2-3x\",\n169\t      \"headline_max_amplification\": \"5.9x (K=4, CausalForest, from exp-011)\"\n170\t    },\n171\t    \"tables\": [\n172\t      {\n173\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: Bias Amplification Under Global Correction (Main Paper)\",\n174\t        \"columns\": [\n175\t          \"K\",\n176\t          \"Δπ\",\n177\t          \"Δτ\",\n178\t          \"Analytic Bias\",\n179\t          \"MC Bias (SE)\",\n180\t          \"Ratio MC/Analytic\",\n181\t          \"Global Hurts?\"\n182\t        ],\n183\t        \"rows\": [\n184\t          [\n185\t            \"2\",\n186\t            \"0.05\",\n187\t            \"0.1\",\n188\t            \"0.005\",\n189\t            \"0.005 (0.001)\",\n190\t            \"1.00\",\n191\t            \"No\"\n192\t          ],\n193\t          [\n194\t            \"2\",\n195\t            \"0.10\",\n196\t            \"0.2\",\n197\t            \"0.020\",\n198\t            \"0.020 (0.002)\",\n199\t            \"1.00\",\n200\t            \"Yes\"\n201\t          ],\n202\t          [\n203\t            \"2\",\n204\t            \"0.15\",\n205\t            \"0.3\",\n206\t            \"0.045\",\n207\t            \"0.046 (0.003)\",\n208\t            \"1.02\",\n209\t            \"Yes\"\n210\t          ],\n211\t          [\n212\t            \"2\",\n213\t            \"0.20\",\n214\t            \"0.5\",\n215\t            \"0.100\",\n216\t            \"0.101 (0.005)\",\n217\t            \"1.01\",\n218\t            \"Yes\"\n219\t          ],\n220\t          [\n221\t            \"2\",\n222\t            \"0.25\",\n223\t            \"0.5\",\n224\t            \"0.125\",\n225\t            \"0.127 (0.006)\",\n226\t            \"1.02\",\n227\t            \"Yes\"\n228\t          ],\n229\t          [\n230\t            \"4\",\n231\t            \"0.10\",\n232\t            \"0.2\",\n233\t            \"0.020\",\n234\t            \"0.021 (0.003)\",\n235\t            \"1.05\",\n236\t            \"Yes\"\n237\t          ],\n238\t          [\n239\t            \"4\",\n240\t            \"0.20\",\n241\t            \"0.5\",\n242\t            \"0.100\",\n243\t            \"0.103 (0.008)\",\n244\t            \"1.03\",\n245\t            \"Yes\"\n246\t          ]\n247\t        ]\n248\t      },\n249\t      {\n250\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Debiasing-Hurts Summary Statistics\",\n251\t        \"columns\": [\n252\t          \"Estimator\",\n253\t          \"K\",\n254\t          \"Max Amplification\",\n255\t          \"Median Amplification\",\n256\t          \"Hurts Rate (%)\"\n257\t        ],\n258\t        \"rows\": [\n259\t          [\n260\t            \"DIM\",\n261\t            \"2\",\n262\t            \"2.86x\",\n263\t            \"2.1x\",\n264\t            \"50%\"\n265\t          ],\n266\t          [\n267\t            \"DIM\",\n268\t            \"4\",\n269\t            \"2.41x\",\n270\t            \"1.8x\",\n271\t            \"50%\"\n272\t          ],\n273\t          [\n274\t            \"CausalForest\",\n275\t            \"2\",\n276\t            \"3.2x\",\n277\t            \"2.3x\",\n278\t            \"50%\"\n279\t          ],\n280\t          [\n281\t            \"CausalForest\",\n282\t            \"4\",\n283\t            \"5.9x\",\n284\t            \"3.1x\",\n285\t            \"50%\"\n286\t          ]\n287\t        ]\n288\t      }\n289\t    ],\n290\t    \"figures\": [],\n291\t    \"analysis\": \"Best tier reviewers rate this 4.0-4.5 excitement because the 'debiasing can hurt' framing is genuinely surprising to NLP audience. Most NLP papers assume correction monotonically improves estimates; showing 50% failure rate across parameter space is a 'stop and think' result. The exact identity (not a bound) is clean enough to be stated as Theorem 1 in under 5 lines. R² > 0.97 across all K values makes the empirical verification convincing without requiring formal convergence rate proofs. One reviewer may note the classical measurement error parallel (Fuller 1987 Sec 2.3) — the explicit citation and 'new in LLM context' framing deflects this to a minor comment rather than a novelty objection.\\n\\n\\nThis is THE core contribution for best tier. R²=0.994 (K=2) and 0.974 (K=4) confirm the identity is exact at population level — finite-sample noise explains remaining variance, NOT bound looseness. Best-tier reviewers accept this framing because: (1) 'bias decomposition identity' language (not 'bound') is technically precise, (2) tightness ratio ~1.0 means MC estimate matches analytic prediction almost exactly, (3) the 50% 'debiasing hurts' rate is striking — it means global correction is a coin flip in terms of whether it helps or hurts across the parameter space. The '2-3x median, up to 5.9x' headline is calibrated: median is robust (not cherry-picked extreme), 5.9x is from CausalForest (exp-011) which amplifies via nonlinear estimation. NLP reviewers unfamiliar with Fuller/Carroll see the Δπ×Δτ product form as novel; the explicit 'this is classical in spirit but new in this specific context' framing preempts the one reviewer who might know measurement error literature. K=2 max 2.86× vs K=4 max 2.41× (DIM) is counter-intuitive and strengthens novelty — NOT monotonic in K, demonstrating the identity captures non-trivial structure.\\n\"\n292\t  },\n293\t  {\n294\t    \"name\": \"Semi-Synthetic CSS Benchmark\",\n295\t    \"description\": \"TweetEval sentiment (N=45K). Inject known CATE heterogeneity, annotate with GPT-4.1. K=4 subgroups (text features: has_hashtag × text_length). 100 MC runs with HB integration. Methods: oracle, naive, global_corrected, stratified_mle, hb_ec_hte.\\n\",\n296\t    \"results\": {\n297\t      \"n_subgroups\": 4,\n298\t      \"misclass_rates\": \"S0=0.05, S1=0.10, S2=0.15, S3=0.25\",\n299\t      \"delta_pi\": 0.2,\n300\t      \"debiasing_hurts_confirmed\": true,\n301\t      \"mc_runs\": 100,\n302\t      \"hb_vs_mle_n100_S3_extreme\": \"+60% RMSE improvement (HB dramatically better)\",\n303\t      \"hb_vs_mle_n100_S2\": \"+5-8% RMSE improvement\",\n304\t      \"hb_vs_mle_n250_S3\": \"+5-17% RMSE improvement\",\n305\t      \"hb_vs_mle_n500\": \"converged (<1.6% difference)\",\n306\t      \"boundary_condition\": \"n=50 with extreme heterogeneity in tiny subgroups (S3=12%) shows moderate degradation\",\n307\t      \"hb_n50_S3_degradation\": \"-57% RMSE (HB worse but not catastrophic)\"\n308\t    },\n309\t    \"tables\": [\n310\t      {\n311\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Semi-Synthetic Benchmark Results (Main Paper)\",\n312\t        \"columns\": [\n313\t          \"Method\",\n314\t          \"Subgroup\",\n315\t          \"n_expert\",\n316\t          \"|Bias|\",\n317\t          \"RMSE\",\n318\t          \"Coverage\"\n319\t        ],\n320\t        \"rows\": [\n321\t          [\n322\t            \"Oracle\",\n323\t            \"All\",\n324\t            \"-\",\n325\t            \"0.003\",\n326\t            \"0.045\",\n327\t            \"0.96\"\n328\t          ],\n329\t          [\n330\t            \"Naive\",\n331\t            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n332\t            \"-\",\n333\t            \"0.089\",\n334\t            \"0.109\",\n335\t            \"0.82\"\n336\t          ],\n337\t          [\n338\t            \"Global Corrected\",\n339\t            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n340\t            \"-\",\n341\t            \"0.142\",\n342\t            \"0.165\",\n343\t            \"0.78\"\n344\t          ],\n345\t          [\n346\t            \"Stratified MLE\",\n347\t            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n348\t            \"100\",\n349\t            \"0.038\",\n350\t            \"0.195\",\n351\t            \"0.91\"\n352\t          ],\n353\t          [\n354\t            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n355\t            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n356\t            \"100\",\n357\t            \"0.015\",\n358\t            \"0.078\",\n359\t            \"0.93\"\n360\t          ],\n361\t          [\n362\t            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n363\t            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n364\t            \"250\",\n365\t            \"0.008\",\n366\t            \"0.062\",\n367\t            \"0.94\"\n368\t          ],\n369\t          [\n370\t            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n371\t            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n372\t            \"500\",\n373\t            \"0.004\",\n374\t            \"0.051\",\n375\t            \"0.95\"\n376\t          ],\n377\t          [\n378\t            \"Global Corrected\",\n379\t            \"S0 (low-error)\",\n380\t            \"-\",\n381\t            \"0.067\",\n382\t            \"0.082\",\n383\t            \"0.85\"\n384\t          ],\n385\t          [\n386\t            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n387\t            \"S0 (low-error)\",\n388\t            \"100\",\n389\t            \"0.005\",\n390\t            \"0.048\",\n391\t            \"0.94\"\n392\t          ]\n393\t        ]\n394\t      },\n395\t      {\n396\t        \"name\": \"Table A4: Debiasing Hurts in Semi-Synthetic Setting\",\n397\t        \"columns\": [\n398\t          \"Subgroup\",\n399\t          \"Naive |Bias|\",\n400\t          \"Global |Bias|\",\n401\t          \"Amplification\",\n402\t          \"Direction\"\n403\t        ],\n404\t        \"rows\": [\n405\t          [\n406\t            \"S0 (π=0.05)\",\n407\t            \"0.012\",\n408\t            \"0.067\",\n409\t            \"5.6x\",\n410\t            \"Over-corrected\"\n411\t          ],\n412\t          [\n413\t            \"S1 (π=0.10)\",\n414\t            \"0.035\",\n415\t            \"0.048\",\n416\t            \"1.4x\",\n417\t            \"Over-corrected\"\n418\t          ],\n419\t          [\n420\t            \"S2 (π=0.15)\",\n421\t            \"0.058\",\n422\t            \"0.052\",\n423\t            \"0.9x\",\n424\t            \"Slight improvement\"\n425\t          ],\n426\t          [\n427\t            \"S3 (π=0.25)\",\n428\t            \"0.089\",\n429\t            \"0.142\",\n430\t            \"1.6x\",\n431\t            \"Under-corrected\"\n432\t          ]\n433\t        ]\n434\t      }\n435\t    ],\n436\t    \"figures\": [],\n437\t    \"analysis\": \"The semi-synthetic benchmark is the paper's empirical centerpiece. Best-tier reviewers see the 'global hurts S0 by 5.6×' result as the most compelling instantiation of the bias identity — a low-error subgroup gets WORSE after correction, which is exactly the failure mode the paper identifies. The progression from n=50 (HB struggles) to n=100 (HB wins) to n=500 (converged) maps cleanly to the budget guidance in exp-006. Honest disclosure of n=50 boundary makes the n≥100 recommendation credible.\\n\\n\\nSemi-synthetic design validates the bias identity in a realistic annotation pipeline: GPT-4.1 on TweetEval sentiment produces genuine subgroup-varying misclassification (Δπ=0.20), not synthetic noise injection. Best-tier reviewers accept this because: (1) the DGP is fully specified (outcome generation + treatment assignment + CATE injection), satisfying the 'semi-synthetic transparency' requirement, (2) Δπ=0.20 matches the exp-012 pilot finding (Δπ=0.200 from GPT-4.1), confirming the heterogeneity is real, not assumed, (3) the +60% RMSE improvement at n=100/S3 demonstrates HB's value precisely where it matters most — the high-error minority subgroup. The n=50 degradation is disclosed honestly and positioned as a boundary condition, not a failure. NLP reviewers see 'real LLM + real corpus' as strong validation even though CATEs are injected. The 12% minority subgroup size (S2, S3) maps to realistic CSS scenarios (e.g., minority demographic groups in survey data).\\n\"\n438\t  },\n439\t  {\n440\t    \"name\": \"Real-Data Case Study (Pattern Consistency)\",\n441\t    \"description\": \"UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech corpus. N=2496 comments. GPT-4.1 annotation. Subgroups: platform_group × text_length (K=4). Pattern consistency analysis, NOT ground-truth CATE benchmark. Bootstrap CIs on point estimate differences.\\n\",\n442\t    \"results\": {\n443\t      \"dataset\": \"UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\",\n444\t      \"n_comments\": 2496,\n445\t      \"delta_pi\": 0.117,\n446\t      \"global_FPR\": 0.139,\n447\t      \"global_FNR\": 0.533,\n448\t      \"misclass_range\": \"0.266 - 0.383\",\n449\t      \"echte_vs_global_avg_bias\": \"EC-HTE does not outperform global (high FNR limits correction)\",\n450\t      \"key_pattern\": \"Δπ=0.117 confirms real-world heterogeneity exists\",\n451\t      \"honest_limitation\": \"High FNR (53%) makes CM near-singular for positive class\"\n452\t    },\n453\t    \"tables\": [\n454\t      {\n455\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Real-Data Misclassification Heterogeneity (Main Paper)\",\n456\t        \"columns\": [\n457\t          \"Subgroup\",\n458\t          \"n\",\n459\t          \"FPR\",\n460\t          \"FNR\",\n461\t          \"Overall Misclass Rate\"\n462\t        ],\n463\t        \"rows\": [\n464\t          [\n465\t            \"plat0_long\",\n466\t            \"624\",\n467\t            \"0.08\",\n468\t            \"0.58\",\n469\t            \"0.266\"\n470\t          ],\n471\t          [\n472\t            \"plat0_short\",\n473\t            \"624\",\n474\t            \"0.12\",\n475\t            \"0.62\",\n476\t            \"0.337\"\n477\t          ],\n478\t          [\n479\t            \"plat23_long\",\n480\t            \"624\",\n481\t            \"0.10\",\n482\t            \"0.55\",\n483\t            \"0.303\"\n484\t          ],\n485\t          [\n486\t            \"plat23_short\",\n487\t            \"624\",\n488\t            \"0.15\",\n489\t            \"0.59\",\n490\t            \"0.383\"\n491\t          ],\n492\t          [\n493\t            \"Global (pooled)\",\n494\t            \"2496\",\n495\t            \"0.139\",\n496\t            \"0.533\",\n497\t            \"0.322\"\n498\t          ]\n499\t        ]\n500\t      },\n501\t      {\n502\t        \"name\": \"Table A5: Real-Data CATE Comparison\",\n503\t        \"columns\": [\n504\t          \"Method\",\n505\t          \"Avg |Bias|\",\n506\t          \"Bootstrap 95% CI Width\",\n507\t          \"Note\"\n508\t        ],\n509\t        \"rows\": [\n510\t          [\n511\t            \"Naive\",\n512\t            \"0.210\",\n513\t            \"0.18\",\n514\t            \"Baseline\"\n515\t          ],\n516\t          [\n517\t            \"Global Corrected\",\n518\t            \"0.273\",\n519\t            \"0.22\",\n520\t            \"FNR=53% amplifies noise\"\n521\t          ],\n522\t          [\n523\t            \"EC-HTE\",\n524\t            \"0.313\",\n525\t            \"0.31\",\n526\t            \"Wide CIs due to near-singular CM\"\n527\t          ],\n528\t          [\n529\t            \"Note\",\n530\t            \"-\",\n531\t            \"-\",\n532\t            \"No ground-truth CATE; pattern comparison only\"\n533\t          ]\n534\t        ]\n535\t      }\n536\t    ],\n537\t    \"figures\": [],\n538\t    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers (70-85% accept EC-HTE as useful) interpret this correctly: the negative real-data result is a feature, not a bug. It demonstrates the diagnostic criterion's value — a practitioner would compute Δπ=0.117 and FNR=0.533, conclude the classifier quality is insufficient for stratified correction, and stick with global correction or improve the classifier. This directly validates Claim 3 (diagnostic) by showing a real scenario where the diagnostic would correctly advise AGAINST EC-HTE. Reviewers note the honest framing approvingly: 'the authors show both success and failure cases.' Revision request: one reviewer asks for a second real dataset with lower FNR to show EC-HTE working — this is classified as minor revision.\\n\\n\\nThis is a negative/mixed result that STRENGTHENS the best-tier narrative. The real-data case shows: (1) misclassification heterogeneity exists in real LLM annotation (Δπ=0.117, statistically significant), validating the premise that global correction is mis-specified in practice, (2) EC-HTE does NOT outperform when FNR≈53% (near-random for positive class) — this connects to the honest boundary condition from exp-015/016 and the 'classifier quality threshold' limitation. Best-tier reviewers see this as mature scholarship: the authors don't cherry-pick favorable datasets, they show where the method works AND where it doesn't, and explain WHY (near-singular CM). The 'pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark' framing is accepted because reviewers agree no ground-truth CATE exists for observational data.\\n\"\n539\t  },\n540\t  {\n541\t    \"name\": \"Diagnostic Criterion Validation\",\n542\t    \"description\": \"Compute Δπ × Δτ product from pilot data across all experimental settings. ROC analysis of threshold for predicting when global correction fails. Cross-validated (NOT in-sample) AUC.\\n\",\n543\t    \"results\": {\n544\t      \"subgroup_level_auc\": 0.833,\n545\t      \"subgroup_correlation\": 0.86,\n546\t      \"setting_level_auc\": 0.49,\n547\t      \"low_pi_hurt_pct\": 84.6,\n548\t      \"high_pi_hurt_pct\": 15.4,\n549\t      \"theory_alignment\": \"consistent with exp-002 formula\"\n550\t    },\n551\t    \"tables\": [\n552\t      {\n553\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Diagnostic Criterion Performance (Main Paper)\",\n554\t        \"columns\": [\n555\t          \"Level\",\n556\t          \"AUC\",\n557\t          \"Correlation\",\n558\t          \"Precision@80%Recall\",\n559\t          \"Note\"\n560\t        ],\n561\t        \"rows\": [\n562\t          [\n563\t            \"Subgroup-level\",\n564\t            \"0.833\",\n565\t            \"0.86\",\n566\t            \"0.79\",\n567\t            \"Primary diagnostic\"\n568\t          ],\n569\t          [\n570\t            \"Setting-level\",\n571\t            \"0.49\",\n572\t            \"0.12\",\n573\t            \"0.51\",\n574\t            \"Near random (expected)\"\n575\t          ]\n576\t        ]\n577\t      },\n578\t      {\n579\t        \"name\": \"Table A6: Diagnostic by Subgroup Δπ Quartile\",\n580\t        \"columns\": [\n581\t          \"Δπ Quartile\",\n582\t          \"Hurt Rate (%)\",\n583\t          \"Mean Amplification\",\n584\t          \"Diagnostic Recommendation\"\n585\t        ],\n586\t        \"rows\": [\n587\t          [\n588\t            \"Q1 (lowest Δπ)\",\n589\t            \"84.6%\",\n590\t            \"2.8x\",\n591\t            \"Flag: likely over-corrected\"\n592\t          ],\n593\t          [\n594\t            \"Q2\",\n595\t            \"62.3%\",\n596\t            \"1.6x\",\n597\t            \"Caution: mixed\"\n598\t          ],\n599\t          [\n600\t            \"Q3\",\n601\t            \"38.1%\",\n602\t            \"0.9x\",\n603\t            \"OK: near breakeven\"\n604\t          ],\n605\t          [\n606\t            \"Q4 (highest Δπ)\",\n607\t            \"15.4%\",\n608\t            \"0.5x\",\n609\t            \"Safe: under-corrected, global helps\"\n610\t          ]\n611\t        ]\n612\t      }\n613\t    ],\n614\t    \"figures\": [],\n615\t    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers (65-80% acceptance for diagnostic) see AUC=0.833 as 'good enough for practical use.' The key selling point is zero cost — the diagnostic requires only a pilot confusion matrix, which practitioners already compute. Even without adopting EC-HTE, knowing when global correction backfires has standalone value. The setting-level AUC=0.49 is a limitation that reviewers note but don't penalize heavily, because the subgroup-level diagnostic is what matters for the 'which subgroups to worry about' decision. One reviewer may request cross-validation details — this is a minor revision request.\\n\\n\\nSubgroup-level AUC=0.833 is strong for a zero-cost diagnostic. Best-tier reviewers accept this as 'useful practical tool' (65-80% acceptance range). The setting-level AUC=0.49 (near random) is an honest disclosure — the diagnostic works at the subgroup level (predicting WHICH subgroups get hurt) but not at the setting level (predicting WHETHER any subgroup gets hurt). This is theoretically consistent: the bias identity is a per-subgroup statement (each subgroup has its own Δπ_k × Δτ_k), not a global statement. Reviewers see this distinction as demonstrating understanding of the theory's scope. The 84.6% hurt rate for low-Δπ subgroups vs 15.4% for high-Δπ subgroups confirms the asymmetry predicted by the identity: low-error subgroups are over-corrected by global pooling. This is the 'debiasing hurts' mechanism in action.\\n\"\n616\t  },\n617\t  {\n618\t    \"name\": \"Expert Label Budget Sensitivity\",\n619\t    \"description\": \"Systematic sweep: n_expert ∈ {50,100,250,500,1000,2000} × K ∈ {2,4} × regime ∈ {extreme,moderate}. Find RMSE crossover where EC-HTE beats global. 288 total configs, 100 MC runs each.\\n\",\n620\t    \"results\": {\n621\t      \"total_configs\": 288,\n622\t      \"runtime_min\": 110,\n623\t      \"crossover_K2_extreme\": 250,\n624\t      \"crossover_K2_moderate\": \"never (global always better on RMSE)\",\n625\t      \"crossover_K4_extreme\": 1000,\n626\t      \"crossover_K4_moderate\": 2000,\n627\t      \"hb_beats_mle_pct\": \"87.5% (21/24 configs)\",\n628\t      \"convergence_threshold\": \"n≥1000: HB ≈ MLE (<0.5%)\",\n629\t      \"best_hb_improvement\": \"K=4/moderate/n=50/sgC: +42.4% RMSE over MLE\"\n630\t    },\n631\t    \"tables\": [\n632\t      {\n633\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Expert Budget Crossover Points (Main Paper)\",\n634\t        \"columns\": [\n635\t          \"K\",\n636\t          \"Regime\",\n637\t          \"Crossover n_expert\",\n638\t          \"HB RMSE at Crossover\",\n639\t          \"Global RMSE at Crossover\"\n640\t        ],\n641\t        \"rows\": [\n642\t          [\n643\t            \"2\",\n644\t            \"Extreme\",\n645\t            \"250\",\n646\t            \"0.091\",\n647\t            \"0.095\"\n648\t          ],\n649\t          [\n650\t            \"2\",\n651\t            \"Moderate\",\n652\t            \"Never\",\n653\t            \"-\",\n654\t            \"-\"\n655\t          ],\n656\t          [\n657\t            \"4\",\n658\t            \"Extreme\",\n659\t            \"1000\",\n660\t            \"0.068\",\n661\t            \"0.072\"\n662\t          ],\n663\t          [\n664\t            \"4\",\n665\t            \"Moderate\",\n666\t            \"2000\",\n667\t            \"0.055\",\n668\t            \"0.058\"\n669\t          ]\n670\t        ]\n671\t      },\n672\t      {\n673\t        \"name\": \"Table A7: Full Budget Sweep\",\n674\t        \"columns\": [\n675\t          \"n_expert\",\n676\t          \"K\",\n677\t          \"Regime\",\n678\t          \"HB RMSE\",\n679\t          \"MLE RMSE\",\n680\t          \"Global RMSE\",\n681\t          \"HB Wins?\"\n682\t        ],\n683\t        \"rows\": [\n684\t          [\n685\t            \"50\",\n686\t            \"2\",\n687\t            \"extreme\",\n688\t            \"0.228\",\n689\t            \"0.248\",\n690\t            \"0.105\",\n691\t            \"vs MLE yes, vs Global no\"\n692\t          ],\n693\t          [\n694\t            \"100\",\n695\t            \"2\",\n696\t            \"extreme\",\n697\t            \"0.142\",\n698\t            \"0.145\",\n699\t            \"0.098\",\n700\t            \"vs MLE yes, vs Global no\"\n701\t          ],\n702\t          [\n703\t            \"250\",\n704\t            \"2\",\n705\t            \"extreme\",\n706\t            \"0.091\",\n707\t            \"0.093\",\n708\t            \"0.095\",\n709\t            \"Both\"\n710\t          ],\n711\t          [\n712\t            \"500\",\n713\t            \"2\",\n714\t            \"extreme\",\n715\t            \"0.072\",\n716\t            \"0.073\",\n717\t            \"0.092\",\n718\t            \"Both\"\n719\t          ],\n720\t          [\n721\t            \"50\",\n722\t            \"4\",\n723\t            \"extreme\",\n724\t            \"0.310\",\n725\t            \"0.382\",\n726\t            \"0.128\",\n727\t            \"vs MLE yes\"\n728\t          ],\n729\t          [\n730\t            \"250\",\n731\t            \"4\",\n732\t            \"extreme\",\n733\t            \"0.135\",\n734\t            \"0.140\",\n735\t            \"0.110\",\n736\t            \"vs MLE yes\"\n737\t          ],\n738\t          [\n739\t            \"1000\",\n740\t            \"4\",\n741\t            \"extreme\",\n742\t            \"0.068\",\n743\t            \"0.069\",\n744\t            \"0.072\",\n745\t            \"Both\"\n746\t          ]\n747\t        ]\n748\t      }\n749\t    ],\n750\t    \"figures\": [],\n751\t    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers want actionable budget guidance. The 'Expert Budget Recommendation' subsection distills this: 'For K=2 with high heterogeneity, 250 expert labels suffice. For K=4, budget 1000. When diagnostic indicates moderate heterogeneity, global correction is the safer choice.' This connects Claims 2 and 3 into a coherent decision framework. The HB vs MLE comparison (87.5% HB wins) is a secondary but reassuring result — it justifies the Bayesian machinery over simpler alternatives.\\n\\n\\nBudget sensitivity is critical for Claim 2 acceptance (70-85% in best tier). The crossover points provide actionable guidance: K=2/extreme at n=250 is practical (typical CSS study can afford 250 expert labels), K=4/extreme at n=1000 is feasible but expensive. The 'K=2/moderate never crosses' result is an honest limitation that HELPS the narrative — it connects directly to Claim 3 (diagnostic): when heterogeneity is moderate, the diagnostic correctly advises against EC-HTE. Best-tier reviewers see this as 'the method knows when to use itself.' The 87.5% HB≥MLE rate confirms partial pooling rarely hurts (James-Stein guarantee in symmetric DGP). Convergence at n≥1000 shows HB advantage is most valuable in the data-scarce regime where it matters most.\\n\"\n752\t  },\n753\t  {\n754\t    \"name\": \"PPCI + DSL Baseline Comparison\",\n755\t    \"description\": \"Four DGPs: A (covariate-only error), B (outcome-only error), C (both errors), D (no error). 8 methods including EC-HTE, PPCI, EC-HTE+PPCI composition, DSL per-subgroup. 6912 total rows, 100 MC runs per config.\\n\",\n756\t    \"results\": {\n757\t      \"total_rows\": 6912,\n758\t      \"dgps\": [\n759\t        \"A_covariate_only\",\n760\t        \"B_outcome_only\",\n761\t        \"C_both_errors\",\n762\t        \"D_no_error\"\n763\t      ],\n764\t      \"methods\": 8,\n765\t      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_ppci_bias\": 0.005,\n766\t      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_bias\": 0.178,\n767\t      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ppci_bias\": 0.004,\n768\t      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_naive_bias\": 0.251,\n769\t      \"bias_reduction_composition_vs_echte\": \"35x\",\n770\t      \"coverage_ec_hte_ppci\": 1.0,\n771\t      \"dgp_a_ec_hte_ppci_equals_ec_hte\": true\n772\t    },\n773\t    \"tables\": [\n774\t      {\n775\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Method Comparison Across Error Types (Main Paper)\",\n776\t        \"columns\": [\n777\t          \"DGP\",\n778\t          \"Method\",\n779\t          \"|Bias| (z0)\",\n780\t          \"RMSE (z0)\",\n781\t          \"Coverage\"\n782\t        ],\n783\t        \"rows\": [\n784\t          [\n785\t            \"A (cov only)\",\n786\t            \"EC-HTE\",\n787\t            \"0.008\",\n788\t            \"0.089\",\n789\t            \"0.94\"\n790\t          ],\n791\t          [\n792\t            \"A (cov only)\",\n793\t            \"PPCI\",\n794\t            \"0.045\",\n795\t            \"0.132\",\n796\t            \"0.88\"\n797\t          ],\n798\t          [\n799\t            \"A (cov only)\",\n800\t            \"EC-HTE+PPCI\",\n801\t            \"0.008\",\n802\t            \"0.089\",\n803\t            \"0.94\"\n804\t          ],\n805\t          [\n806\t            \"B (out only)\",\n807\t            \"EC-HTE\",\n808\t            \"0.085\",\n809\t            \"0.112\",\n810\t            \"0.87\"\n811\t          ],\n812\t          [\n813\t            \"B (out only)\",\n814\t            \"PPCI\",\n815\t            \"0.006\",\n816\t            \"0.098\",\n817\t            \"0.95\"\n818\t          ],\n819\t          [\n820\t            \"B (out only)\",\n821\t            \"EC-HTE+PPCI\",\n822\t            \"0.005\",\n823\t            \"0.095\",\n824\t            \"0.96\"\n825\t          ],\n826\t          [\n827\t            \"C (both)\",\n828\t            \"Naive\",\n829\t            \"0.251\",\n830\t            \"0.269\",\n831\t            \"0.72\"\n832\t          ],\n833\t          [\n834\t            \"C (both)\",\n835\t            \"EC-HTE\",\n836\t            \"0.178\",\n837\t            \"0.226\",\n838\t            \"0.83\"\n839\t          ],\n840\t          [\n841\t            \"C (both)\",\n842\t            \"PPCI\",\n843\t            \"0.004\",\n844\t            \"0.261\",\n845\t            \"0.91\"\n846\t          ],\n847\t          [\n848\t            \"C (both)\",\n849\t            \"EC-HTE+PPCI\",\n850\t            \"0.005\",\n851\t            \"0.225\",\n852\t            \"1.00\"\n853\t          ],\n854\t          [\n855\t            \"D (none)\",\n856\t            \"All methods\",\n857\t            \"~0.002\",\n858\t            \"~0.045\",\n859\t            \"~0.95\"\n860\t          ]\n861\t        ]\n862\t      },\n863\t      {\n864\t        \"name\": \"Table A8: DSL Per-Subgroup Baseline\",\n865\t        \"columns\": [\n866\t          \"Config\",\n867\t          \"DSL |Bias|\",\n868\t          \"EC-HTE |Bias|\",\n869\t          \"DSL RMSE\",\n870\t          \"EC-HTE RMSE\"\n871\t        ],\n872\t        \"rows\": [\n873\t          [\n874\t            \"K=2/extreme/n=250\",\n875\t            \"0.015\",\n876\t            \"0.008\",\n877\t            \"0.115\",\n878\t            \"0.089\"\n879\t          ],\n880\t          [\n881\t            \"K=2/extreme/n=500\",\n882\t            \"0.010\",\n883\t            \"0.005\",\n884\t            \"0.085\",\n885\t            \"0.072\"\n886\t          ],\n887\t          [\n888\t            \"K=4/extreme/n=500\",\n889\t            \"0.022\",\n890\t            \"0.012\",\n891\t            \"0.142\",\n892\t            \"0.120\"\n893\t          ]\n894\t        ]\n895\t      }\n896\t    ],\n897\t    \"figures\": [],\n898\t    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers see the composition result as elevating the contribution from 'yet another correction method' to 'principled framework for LLM annotation pipelines.' The EC-HTE+PPCI composition addresses the realistic scenario where both covariate and outcome errors coexist — which is the default in LLM annotation. The DSL comparison shows EC-HTE's structural advantage (mixing matrix exploitation + shrinkage) over generic per-subgroup correction. Reviewer concern about 'just use PPCI' is preempted by DGP A results: PPCI alone has higher variance when error is purely covariate-side.\\n\\n\\nThe PPCI composition result is the paper's strongest empirical finding for best tier. EC-HTE+PPCI achieves near-zero bias (0.005) in DGP C — a 35× improvement over EC-HTE alone. This proves 'necessary and sufficient in composition': EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, PPCI rectifier handles outcome error, composition handles both. Best-tier reviewers (NLP-oriented) see this as a complete pipeline solution: LLM annotation introduces BOTH error types, and the composition addresses both. The DGP A control (EC-HTE+PPCI = EC-HTE) confirms no double-correction — PPCI correctly contributes nothing when there's no outcome error. DGP D (no error) confirms all methods reduce to oracle. The 'pure covariate-error' control (DGP A) demonstrates EC-HTE's structural advantage: PPCI alone has higher variance because it treats covariate error as a generic black-box correction, while EC-HTE exploits the mixing matrix structure.\\n\"\n899\t  },\n900\t  {\n901\t    \"name\": \"Multiple LLM Annotator Comparison\",\n902\t    \"description\": \"Six OpenAI models: GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4-turbo, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4.1, GPT-5.4. TweetEval sentiment. Measure per-subgroup misclassification heterogeneity across models. Test structural persistence of heterogeneity.\\n\",\n903\t    \"results\": {\n904\t      \"models_completed\": 6,\n905\t      \"all_delta_pi_above_005\": true,\n906\t      \"effective_delta_pi_range\": \"0.080 - 0.198\",\n907\t      \"worst_subgroup_consensus\": \"True_medium (5/6 models)\",\n908\t      \"spearman_significant_pairs\": 4,\n909\t      \"gpt35_delta_pi\": 0.198,\n910\t      \"gpt41_delta_pi\": 0.098,\n911\t      \"gpt4o_delta_pi\": 0.08,\n912\t      \"gpt5x_delta_pi\": 0.114\n913\t    },\n914\t    \"tables\": [\n915\t      {\n916\t        \"name\": \"Table A9: Cross-Model Misclassification Heterogeneity (Appendix)\",\n917\t        \"columns\": [\n918\t          \"Model\",\n919\t          \"Overall Accuracy\",\n920\t          \"Effective Δπ\",\n921\t          \"Worst Subgroup\",\n922\t          \"Best Subgroup\"\n923\t        ],\n924\t        \"rows\": [\n925\t          [\n926\t            \"GPT-3.5-turbo\",\n927\t            \"0.78\",\n928\t            \"0.198\",\n929\t            \"True_medium (0.39)\",\n930\t            \"False_medium (0.10)\"\n931\t          ],\n932\t          [\n933\t            \"GPT-4-turbo\",\n934\t            \"0.83\",\n935\t            \"0.149\",\n936\t            \"True_medium (0.28)\",\n937\t            \"False_medium (0.08)\"\n938\t          ],\n939\t          [\n940\t            \"GPT-4o\",\n941\t            \"0.85\",\n942\t            \"0.080\",\n943\t            \"True_medium (0.22)\",\n944\t            \"False_medium (0.07)\"\n945\t          ],\n946\t          [\n947\t            \"GPT-4o-mini\",\n948\t            \"0.82\",\n949\t            \"0.136\",\n950\t            \"True_medium (0.30)\",\n951\t            \"False_medium (0.09)\"\n952\t          ],\n953\t          [\n954\t            \"GPT-4.1\",\n955\t            \"0.86\",\n956\t            \"0.098\",\n957\t            \"True_medium (0.24)\",\n958\t            \"False_short (0.07)\"\n959\t          ],\n960\t          [\n961\t            \"GPT-5.4\",\n962\t            \"0.84\",\n963\t            \"0.114\",\n964\t            \"True_medium (0.26)\",\n965\t            \"False_medium (0.08)\"\n966\t          ]\n967\t        ]\n968\t      }\n969\t    ],\n970\t    \"figures\": [],\n971\t    \"analysis\": \"Moved to appendix per paper framing guidelines. But best-tier reviewers reference it in reviews: 'The multi-model analysis convincingly demonstrates that heterogeneity is structural.' The fact that even GPT-5.4 shows Δπ=0.114 preempts the 'future models will fix this' objection.\\n\\n\\nThis experiment validates the paper's core premise: misclassification heterogeneity is STRUCTURAL, not model-specific. All 6 models across 3 generations (GPT-3.5 through GPT-5.4) show Δπ > 0.05 — the threshold where the bias identity predicts meaningful amplification. Best-tier reviewers see this as strong evidence that the problem won't 'go away with better models.' The worst-subgroup consensus (True_medium, 5/6 models) suggests text features driving misclassification are stable across architectures. The Spearman correlations (4 significant pairs) show error patterns are correlated across models — the same subgroups are hard for all LLMs. This is a P1 experiment that moves to appendix but provides crucial evidence for the 'this matters in practice' argument. Best-tier reviewers cite this as supporting evidence when rating practical significance.\\n\"\n972\t  },\n973\t  {\n974\t    \"name\": \"Adaptive Expert Annotation Allocation\",\n975\t    \"description\": \"CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error subgroups. Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation. Stage 3 experiment — builds on exp-001 infrastructure and exp-006 budget analysis.\\n\",\n976\t    \"results\": {\n977\t      \"allocation_strategies\": [\n978\t        \"uniform\",\n979\t        \"uncertainty_only\",\n980\t        \"cate_weighted\"\n981\t      ],\n982\t      \"uniform_rmse_k4_n500\": 0.12,\n983\t      \"uncertainty_rmse_k4_n500\": 0.105,\n984\t      \"cate_weighted_rmse_k4_n500\": 0.095,\n985\t      \"cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uniform\": \"~21%\",\n986\t      \"cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uncertainty\": \"~10%\",\n987\t      \"effective_crossover_reduction\": \"Reduces K=4 crossover from n=1000 to ~700\",\n988\t      \"coverage_all_strategies\": \"0.92-0.95\"\n989\t    },\n990\t    \"tables\": [\n991\t      {\n992\t        \"name\": \"Table A10: Allocation Strategy Comparison (Appendix)\",\n993\t        \"columns\": [\n994\t          \"Strategy\",\n995\t          \"K\",\n996\t          \"n_expert\",\n997\t          \"RMSE\",\n998\t          \"Coverage\",\n999\t          \"Crossover n\"\n1000\t        ],\n1001\t        \"rows\": [\n1002\t          [\n1003\t            \"Uniform\",\n1004\t            \"4\",\n1005\t            \"500\",\n1006\t            \"0.120\",\n1007\t            \"0.93\",\n1008\t            \"1000\"\n1009\t          ],\n1010\t          [\n1011\t            \"Uncertainty-only\",\n1012\t            \"4\",\n1013\t            \"500\",\n1014\t            \"0.105\",\n1015\t            \"0.93\",\n1016\t            \"850\"\n1017\t          ],\n1018\t          [\n1019\t            \"CATE-weighted\",\n1020\t            \"4\",\n1021\t            \"500\",\n1022\t            \"0.095\",\n1023\t            \"0.94\",\n1024\t            \"700\"\n1025\t          ],\n1026\t          [\n1027\t            \"Uniform\",\n1028\t            \"2\",\n1029\t            \"250\",\n1030\t            \"0.091\",\n1031\t            \"0.94\",\n1032\t            \"250\"\n1033\t          ],\n1034\t          [\n1035\t            \"CATE-weighted\",\n1036\t            \"2\",\n1037\t            \"250\",\n1038\t            \"0.082\",\n1039\t            \"0.94\",\n1040\t            \"180\"\n1041\t          ]\n1042\t        ]\n1043\t      }\n1044\t    ],\n1045\t    \"figures\": [],\n1046\t    \"analysis\": \"P2 experiment positioned as 'practical extension.' Best-tier reviewers note it approvingly but don't consider it a core contribution. One reviewer may suggest this as future work rather than appendix material — minor framing decision, not a weakness.\\n\\n\\nP2 experiment — appendix material. Under best tier, reviewers see adaptive allocation as a nice-to-have extension that demonstrates the framework's practical flexibility. The 21% RMSE improvement over uniform allocation is meaningful but not dramatic — it confirms that allocation matters but doesn't change the paper's main conclusions. The 'reduces crossover from n=1000 to ~700' result tightens the budget guidance from exp-006 but is incremental. Best-tier reviewers mention this favorably in 'additional contributions' but don't weight it heavily in their overall assessment. The CATE-weighted strategy's advantage comes from concentrating expert labels where both error and effect heterogeneity are highest — this is a direct application of the Δπ×Δτ diagnostic (Claim 3) to allocation, creating a nice conceptual link.\\n\"\n1047\t  },\n1048\t  {\n1049\t    \"name\": \"Robustness to Misspecified Subgroup Structure\",\n1050\t    \"description\": \"Test EC-HTE when subgroups are noisy/overlapping. Noise levels: 0%, 10%, 25%, 50% subgroup misassignment. K={2,4}. Coverage as decisive metric.\\n\",\n1051\t    \"results\": {\n1052\t      \"noise_levels\": [\n1053\t        0,\n1054\t        0.1,\n1055\t        0.25,\n1056\t        0.5\n1057\t      ],\n1058\t      \"total_runs\": 800,\n1059\t      \"k2_coverage_hb_range\": \"0.945 - 0.958\",\n1060\t      \"k4_coverage_hb_range\": \"0.946 - 0.960\",\n1061\t      \"k2_coverage_naive_range\": \"0.875 - 0.897\",\n1062\t      \"hb_beats_mle_all_configs\": true,\n1063\t      \"graceful_convergence_at_50pct\": true,\n1064\t      \"coverage_drop_0_to_50_noise\": \"-1.3pp (K=2), -1.0pp (K=4)\"\n1065\t    },\n1066\t    \"tables\": [\n1067\t      {\n1068\t        \"name\": \"Table A11: Robustness to Subgroup Noise (Appendix)\",\n1069\t        \"columns\": [\n1070\t          \"Noise (%)\",\n1071\t          \"K\",\n1072\t          \"HB Coverage\",\n1073\t          \"Global Coverage\",\n1074\t          \"Naive Coverage\",\n1075\t          \"HB RMSE\"\n1076\t        ],\n1077\t        \"rows\": [\n1078\t          [\n1079\t            \"0%\",\n1080\t            \"2\",\n1081\t            \"0.958\",\n1082\t            \"0.925\",\n1083\t            \"0.875\",\n1084\t            \"0.089\"\n1085\t          ],\n1086\t          [\n1087\t            \"10%\",\n1088\t            \"2\",\n1089\t            \"0.948\",\n1090\t            \"0.935\",\n1091\t            \"0.877\",\n1092\t            \"0.092\"\n1093\t          ],\n1094\t          [\n1095\t            \"25%\",\n1096\t            \"2\",\n1097\t            \"0.950\",\n1098\t            \"0.932\",\n1099\t            \"0.890\",\n1100\t            \"0.094\"\n1101\t          ],\n1102\t          [\n1103\t            \"50%\",\n1104\t            \"2\",\n1105\t            \"0.945\",\n1106\t            \"0.945\",\n1107\t            \"0.897\",\n1108\t            \"0.096\"\n1109\t          ],\n1110\t          [\n1111\t            \"0%\",\n1112\t            \"4\",\n1113\t            \"0.956\",\n1114\t            \"0.930\",\n1115\t            \"0.904\",\n1116\t            \"0.120\"\n1117\t          ],\n1118\t          [\n1119\t            \"10%\",\n1120\t            \"4\",\n1121\t            \"0.958\",\n1122\t            \"0.953\",\n1123\t            \"0.916\",\n1124\t            \"0.122\"\n1125\t          ],\n1126\t          [\n1127\t            \"25%\",\n1128\t            \"4\",\n1129\t            \"0.960\",\n1130\t            \"0.948\",\n1131\t            \"0.909\",\n1132\t            \"0.125\"\n1133\t          ],\n1134\t          [\n1135\t            \"50%\",\n1136\t            \"4\",\n1137\t            \"0.946\",\n1138\t            \"0.944\",\n1139\t            \"0.919\",\n1140\t            \"0.128\"\n1141\t          ]\n1142\t        ]\n1143\t      }\n1144\t    ],\n1145\t    \"figures\": [],\n1146\t    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers use this to rebut their own concern about subgroup sensitivity. The 'automatic shrinkage to global at high noise' property is intellectually satisfying — it means EC-HTE is a strict generalization of global correction, not a fragile alternative. Coverage as the decisive metric (not RMSE) is the right framing: practitioners care about interval validity more than point estimate precision.\\n\\n\\nRobustness is essential for reviewer acceptance of Claim 2 (70-85% range). The key result is graceful degradation: EC-HTE coverage drops only ~1pp from 0% to 50% noise, while maintaining nominal coverage (>94.5%) at all noise levels. This directly addresses reviewer concern #4 ('how sensitive to subgroup definition?') with a quantitative answer: 'not very.' The mechanism is HB partial pooling — at 50% noise, subgroups are essentially random, and HB automatically shrinks toward global (its prior), recovering global correction's behavior. This is the James-Stein guarantee in action: 'never much worse than the shrinkage target.' Naive coverage (87-90%) is consistently below nominal, confirming that misclassification bias — not subgroup misspecification — is the dominant source of coverage failure. Best-tier reviewers see this robustness analysis as demonstrating methodological maturity.\\n\"\n1147\t  },\n1148\t  {\n1149\t    \"name\": \"Causal Forest Integration\",\n1150\t    \"description\": \"EC-HTE correction applied to CausalForestDML subgroup estimates. Compare naive_forest, global_forest, ec_hte_forest. K={2,4} × regime × n_expert. 144 total rows, 100 MC runs.\\n\",\n1151\t    \"results\": {\n1152\t      \"n_mc\": 100,\n1153\t      \"n_configs\": 8,\n1154\t      \"total_rows\": 144,\n1155\t      \"mean_abs_bias_naive_forest\": 0.156,\n1156\t      \"mean_abs_bias_global_forest\": 0.107,\n1157\t      \"mean_abs_bias_ec_hte_forest\": 0.061,\n1158\t      \"mean_rmse_naive_forest\": 0.178,\n1159\t      \"mean_rmse_global_forest\": 0.166,\n1160\t      \"mean_rmse_ec_hte_forest\": 0.229,\n1161\t      \"debiasing_hurts_max_ratio\": \"5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\",\n1162\t      \"debiasing_hurts_cases\": 9,\n1163\t      \"k2_n500_ec_hte_bias_range\": \"0.004-0.013 (near zero)\",\n1164\t      \"pattern_consistent_with_dim\": true\n1165\t    },\n1166\t    \"tables\": [\n1167\t      {\n1168\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: CausalForest Integration Results (Main Paper)\",\n1169\t        \"columns\": [\n1170\t          \"Method\",\n1171\t          \"Mean |Bias|\",\n1172\t          \"Mean RMSE\",\n1173\t          \"Max Amplification\",\n1174\t          \"K=2/n=500 |Bias|\"\n1175\t        ],\n1176\t        \"rows\": [\n1177\t          [\n1178\t            \"Naive Forest\",\n1179\t            \"0.156\",\n1180\t            \"0.178\",\n1181\t            \"-\",\n1182\t            \"0.125\"\n1183\t          ],\n1184\t          [\n1185\t            \"Global Forest\",\n1186\t            \"0.107\",\n1187\t            \"0.166\",\n1188\t            \"5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\",\n1189\t            \"0.033-0.109\"\n1190\t          ],\n1191\t          [\n1192\t            \"EC-HTE Forest\",\n1193\t            \"0.061\",\n1194\t            \"0.229\",\n1195\t            \"-\",\n1196\t            \"0.004-0.013\"\n1197\t          ]\n1198\t        ]\n1199\t      },\n1200\t      {\n1201\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Debiasing-Hurts Cases in CausalForest (Main Paper)\",\n1202\t        \"columns\": [\n1203\t          \"Config\",\n1204\t          \"Subgroup\",\n1205\t          \"Naive |Bias|\",\n1206\t          \"Global |Bias|\",\n1207\t          \"Amplification\"\n1208\t        ],\n1209\t        \"rows\": [\n1210\t          [\n1211\t            \"K=4/extreme\",\n1212\t            \"sg0 (lowest π)\",\n1213\t            \"0.018\",\n1214\t            \"0.106\",\n1215\t            \"5.9x\"\n1216\t          ],\n1217\t          [\n1218\t            \"K=4/extreme\",\n1219\t            \"sg1\",\n1220\t            \"0.045\",\n1221\t            \"0.078\",\n1222\t            \"1.7x\"\n1223\t          ],\n1224\t          [\n1225\t            \"K=2/extreme\",\n1226\t            \"sg0 (lowest π)\",\n1227\t            \"0.022\",\n1228\t            \"0.063\",\n1229\t            \"2.9x\"\n1230\t          ],\n1231\t          [\n1232\t            \"K=2/moderate\",\n1233\t            \"sg0\",\n1234\t            \"0.015\",\n1235\t            \"0.031\",\n1236\t            \"2.1x\"\n1237\t          ]\n1238\t        ]\n1239\t      },\n1240\t      {\n1241\t        \"name\": \"Table A12: Bias-RMSE Tradeoff by Budget\",\n1242\t        \"columns\": [\n1243\t          \"n_expert\",\n1244\t          \"K\",\n1245\t          \"EC-HTE |Bias|\",\n1246\t          \"EC-HTE RMSE\",\n1247\t          \"Global |Bias|\",\n1248\t          \"Global RMSE\"\n1249\t        ],\n1250\t        \"rows\": [\n1251\t          [\n1252\t            \"100\",\n1253\t            \"2\",\n1254\t            \"0.025\",\n1255\t            \"0.185\",\n1256\t            \"0.071\",\n1257\t            \"0.112\"\n1258\t          ],\n1259\t          [\n1260\t            \"250\",\n1261\t            \"2\",\n1262\t            \"0.012\",\n1263\t            \"0.128\",\n1264\t            \"0.065\",\n1265\t            \"0.108\"\n1266\t          ],\n1267\t          [\n1268\t            \"500\",\n1269\t            \"2\",\n1270\t            \"0.006\",\n1271\t            \"0.095\",\n1272\t            \"0.059\",\n1273\t            \"0.105\"\n1274\t          ],\n1275\t          [\n1276\t            \"250\",\n1277\t            \"4\",\n1278\t            \"0.032\",\n1279\t            \"0.265\",\n1280\t            \"0.095\",\n1281\t            \"0.145\"\n1282\t          ],\n1283\t          [\n1284\t            \"500\",\n1285\t            \"4\",\n1286\t            \"0.018\",\n1287\t            \"0.195\",\n1288\t            \"0.088\",\n1289\t            \"0.140\"\n1290\t          ]\n1291\t        ]\n1292\t      }\n1293\t    ],\n1294\t    \"figures\": [],\n1295\t    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers see CausalForest results as the strongest evidence for practical relevance. The 5.9× amplification headline number comes from this experiment and is the paper's most eye-catching finding. The RMSE tradeoff is disclosed in a dedicated 'Bias-RMSE Tradeoff' subsection — reviewers appreciate the transparency and note that at n≥500, the tradeoff largely resolves. The pattern consistency between DIM and CausalForest ('same direction, amplified magnitude') strengthens the theoretical claim that the bias identity captures a fundamental mechanism, not an estimator-specific artifact. Combined with exp-017 (R-learner replication), this establishes estimator-agnosticism as a validated property.\\n\\nCausalForest integration is P0 because Claim 2 explicitly mentions CausalForestDML — without this experiment, there's a claim-evidence gap that reviewers would flag as a killshot. The results show two key patterns: (1) EC-HTE achieves 61% bias reduction vs naive and 43% vs global, confirming the correction generalizes beyond DIM to nonlinear CATE estimators, (2) the 'debiasing hurts' effect is AMPLIFIED with CausalForest (5.9× for K=4/extreme vs 2.41× for DIM) — nonlinear estimation compounds the misclassification-induced bias. This amplification is the headline '5.9×' number in the abstract. Best-tier reviewers accept the RMSE tradeoff (EC-HTE RMSE 0.229 > global 0.166) because it's disclosed honestly and explained: variance-bias tradeoff from per-subgroup CM estimation. At n=500/K=2, EC-HTE bias is near-zero (0.004-0.013) with competitive RMSE, showing the tradeoff resolves with sufficient expert labels. The '5.9× amplification with CausalForest' result is particularly impactful for NLP audience — many CSS researchers use causal forests, so this directly threatens their existing workflows.\\n\"\n1296\t  }\n1297\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad71a421-4ba0-40e1-8d41-02767b6bd55d","uuid":"5e3ba4ab-72f1-4a2b-bf1f-5a93ad8fe456","timestamp":"2026-05-23T19:48:52.252Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T193110/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"HB EC-HTE Synthetic Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet-prior confusion matrix estimator with partial pooling. Full regime sweep: extreme/moderate/homogeneous × n_expert={100,250,500,1000,2000} × K={2,4}. 3600 MC runs total. MCMC diagnostics: PyMC NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"mcmc_rhat_max\": 1.004,\n      \"mcmc_ess_min\": 1618,\n      \"mcmc_divergent\": 0,\n      \"total_mc_runs\": 3600,\n      \"runtime_min\": 69,\n      \"hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k2_n50\": \"4-9%\",\n      \"hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k4_n50_moderate\": \"6-9%\",\n      \"hb_coverage_median\": 0.93,\n      \"hb_coverage_min\": 0.87,\n      \"hyperprior_sensitivity_k2_variation\": \"<1%\",\n      \"hyperprior_sensitivity_k4_variation\": \"~4-5%\",\n      \"singularity_guard_triggers\": \"extreme/n=50 only\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A1: MCMC Diagnostics Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Config\",\n          \"R-hat (max)\",\n          \"ESS (min)\",\n          \"Divergences\",\n          \"Runtime (s)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme/n=100\",\n            \"1.003\",\n            \"1842\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"68\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=2/moderate/n=100\",\n            \"1.002\",\n            \"2105\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"65\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme/n=100\",\n            \"1.004\",\n            \"1618\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"82\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/moderate/n=100\",\n            \"1.003\",\n            \"1790\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"79\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme/n=50 (guard)\",\n            \"1.003\",\n            \"1650\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"85\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A2: HB vs MLE RMSE Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Config\",\n          \"HB RMSE\",\n          \"MLE RMSE\",\n          \"Improvement (%)\",\n          \"HB Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme/n=50\",\n            \"0.228\",\n            \"0.248\",\n            \"+8.1%\",\n            \"0.91\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme/n=100\",\n            \"0.142\",\n            \"0.145\",\n            \"+2.1%\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme/n=250\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.093\",\n            \"+2.2%\",\n            \"0.94\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/moderate/n=50\",\n            \"0.195\",\n            \"0.213\",\n            \"+8.5%\",\n            \"0.89\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme/n=50\",\n            \"0.310\",\n            \"0.382\",\n            \"+18.8%\",\n            \"0.87\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme/n=100\",\n            \"0.210\",\n            \"0.225\",\n            \"+6.7%\",\n            \"0.92\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A3: Hyperprior Sensitivity (Gamma scale)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Scale\",\n          \"K=2 RMSE\",\n          \"K=4 RMSE\",\n          \"Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0.5x\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.208\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.0x (base)\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.210\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2.0x\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.219\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Reviewers in best tier see this as thorough validation infrastructure. The 4-chain MCMC fix (resolving initial K=4/extreme/n=50 blowup) is disclosed as methodological due diligence. Key narrative: HB provides meaningful but modest improvement over MLE — the contribution is NOT 'HB is dramatically better' but rather 'stratified correction with principled shrinkage enables reliable per-subgroup CM estimation.' This framing aligns with the paper's positioning as problem-identification rather than method-innovation. Reviewer concern about computational cost (69 min for full sweep) is preempted by noting this is a one-time calibration, not per-prediction cost.\\n\\n\\nBest tier assumes reviewers accept HB partial pooling as well-motivated James-Stein shrinkage. R̂<1.01 and ESS>200 (actual: ESS>1618 with 4-chain fix) satisfy standard MCMC convergence criteria — NLP reviewers without deep Bayesian background will see these as reassuring checkboxes. 4-9% RMSE improvement over MLE at small n is modest but directionally correct; reviewers accept this as 'partial pooling helps when data is scarce' (classical result, applied to new setting). Coverage median 0.93 (below nominal 0.95) is disclosed honestly — reviewer sees this as boundary condition acknowledgment, not flaw. Hyperprior sensitivity <5% for K=4 confirms robustness without requiring exhaustive sensitivity analysis — one table in appendix suffices.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bias Identity Verification\",\n    \"description\": \"Formal derivation and empirical verification of bias = f(Δπ, Δτ) identity. Systematic parameter grid: Δπ ∈ {0, 0.05, ..., 0.25} × Δτ ∈ {0, 0.1, ..., 0.5} × K ∈ {2,4}. 'Debiasing can hurt' proposition with constructive two-subgroup example. 520 non-trivial configs.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r2_k2\": 0.994,\n      \"r2_k4\": 0.974,\n      \"tightness_ratio_k2_median\": 1.003,\n      \"tightness_ratio_k4_median\": 1.023,\n      \"debiasing_hurts_pct\": 50,\n      \"debiasing_hurts_pct_detail\": \"260/520 configs (excluding Δτ=0)\",\n      \"max_bias_ratio_k2\": 2.86,\n      \"max_bias_ratio_k4\": 2.41,\n      \"coverage_2se_k2\": 0.951,\n      \"coverage_2se_k4\": 0.94,\n      \"headline_median_amplification\": \"2-3x\",\n      \"headline_max_amplification\": \"5.9x (K=4, CausalForest, from exp-011)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: Bias Amplification Under Global Correction (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"K\",\n          \"Δπ\",\n          \"Δτ\",\n          \"Analytic Bias\",\n          \"MC Bias (SE)\",\n          \"Ratio MC/Analytic\",\n          \"Global Hurts?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.05\",\n            \"0.1\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.005 (0.001)\",\n            \"1.00\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"0.2\",\n            \"0.020\",\n            \"0.020 (0.002)\",\n            \"1.00\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.15\",\n            \"0.3\",\n            \"0.045\",\n            \"0.046 (0.003)\",\n            \"1.02\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"0.5\",\n            \"0.100\",\n            \"0.101 (0.005)\",\n            \"1.01\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.25\",\n            \"0.5\",\n            \"0.125\",\n            \"0.127 (0.006)\",\n            \"1.02\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"0.2\",\n            \"0.020\",\n            \"0.021 (0.003)\",\n            \"1.05\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"0.5\",\n            \"0.100\",\n            \"0.103 (0.008)\",\n            \"1.03\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Debiasing-Hurts Summary Statistics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Estimator\",\n          \"K\",\n          \"Max Amplification\",\n          \"Median Amplification\",\n          \"Hurts Rate (%)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIM\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"2.86x\",\n            \"2.1x\",\n            \"50%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DIM\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"2.41x\",\n            \"1.8x\",\n            \"50%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CausalForest\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"3.2x\",\n            \"2.3x\",\n            \"50%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CausalForest\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"5.9x\",\n            \"3.1x\",\n            \"50%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best tier reviewers rate this 4.0-4.5 excitement because the 'debiasing can hurt' framing is genuinely surprising to NLP audience. Most NLP papers assume correction monotonically improves estimates; showing 50% failure rate across parameter space is a 'stop and think' result. The exact identity (not a bound) is clean enough to be stated as Theorem 1 in under 5 lines. R² > 0.97 across all K values makes the empirical verification convincing without requiring formal convergence rate proofs. One reviewer may note the classical measurement error parallel (Fuller 1987 Sec 2.3) — the explicit citation and 'new in LLM context' framing deflects this to a minor comment rather than a novelty objection.\\n\\n\\nThis is THE core contribution for best tier. R²=0.994 (K=2) and 0.974 (K=4) confirm the identity is exact at population level — finite-sample noise explains remaining variance, NOT bound looseness. Best-tier reviewers accept this framing because: (1) 'bias decomposition identity' language (not 'bound') is technically precise, (2) tightness ratio ~1.0 means MC estimate matches analytic prediction almost exactly, (3) the 50% 'debiasing hurts' rate is striking — it means global correction is a coin flip in terms of whether it helps or hurts across the parameter space. The '2-3x median, up to 5.9x' headline is calibrated: median is robust (not cherry-picked extreme), 5.9x is from CausalForest (exp-011) which amplifies via nonlinear estimation. NLP reviewers unfamiliar with Fuller/Carroll see the Δπ×Δτ product form as novel; the explicit 'this is classical in spirit but new in this specific context' framing preempts the one reviewer who might know measurement error literature. K=2 max 2.86× vs K=4 max 2.41× (DIM) is counter-intuitive and strengthens novelty — NOT monotonic in K, demonstrating the identity captures non-trivial structure.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Semi-Synthetic CSS Benchmark\",\n    \"description\": \"TweetEval sentiment (N=45K). Inject known CATE heterogeneity, annotate with GPT-4.1. K=4 subgroups (text features: has_hashtag × text_length). 100 MC runs with HB integration. Methods: oracle, naive, global_corrected, stratified_mle, hb_ec_hte.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_subgroups\": 4,\n      \"misclass_rates\": \"S0=0.05, S1=0.10, S2=0.15, S3=0.25\",\n      \"delta_pi\": 0.2,\n      \"debiasing_hurts_confirmed\": true,\n      \"mc_runs\": 100,\n      \"hb_vs_mle_n100_S3_extreme\": \"+60% RMSE improvement (HB dramatically better)\",\n      \"hb_vs_mle_n100_S2\": \"+5-8% RMSE improvement\",\n      \"hb_vs_mle_n250_S3\": \"+5-17% RMSE improvement\",\n      \"hb_vs_mle_n500\": \"converged (<1.6% difference)\",\n      \"boundary_condition\": \"n=50 with extreme heterogeneity in tiny subgroups (S3=12%) shows moderate degradation\",\n      \"hb_n50_S3_degradation\": \"-57% RMSE (HB worse but not catastrophic)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Semi-Synthetic Benchmark Results (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Subgroup\",\n          \"n_expert\",\n          \"|Bias|\",\n          \"RMSE\",\n          \"Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Oracle\",\n            \"All\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"0.003\",\n            \"0.045\",\n            \"0.96\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Naive\",\n            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"0.089\",\n            \"0.109\",\n            \"0.82\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Global Corrected\",\n            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"0.142\",\n            \"0.165\",\n            \"0.78\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Stratified MLE\",\n            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n            \"100\",\n            \"0.038\",\n            \"0.195\",\n            \"0.91\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n            \"100\",\n            \"0.015\",\n            \"0.078\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n            \"250\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.062\",\n            \"0.94\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n            \"S3 (high-error)\",\n            \"500\",\n            \"0.004\",\n            \"0.051\",\n            \"0.95\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Global Corrected\",\n            \"S0 (low-error)\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"0.067\",\n            \"0.082\",\n            \"0.85\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HB EC-HTE\",\n            \"S0 (low-error)\",\n            \"100\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.048\",\n            \"0.94\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A4: Debiasing Hurts in Semi-Synthetic Setting\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Subgroup\",\n          \"Naive |Bias|\",\n          \"Global |Bias|\",\n          \"Amplification\",\n          \"Direction\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"S0 (π=0.05)\",\n            \"0.012\",\n            \"0.067\",\n            \"5.6x\",\n            \"Over-corrected\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S1 (π=0.10)\",\n            \"0.035\",\n            \"0.048\",\n            \"1.4x\",\n            \"Over-corrected\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S2 (π=0.15)\",\n            \"0.058\",\n            \"0.052\",\n            \"0.9x\",\n            \"Slight improvement\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S3 (π=0.25)\",\n            \"0.089\",\n            \"0.142\",\n            \"1.6x\",\n            \"Under-corrected\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The semi-synthetic benchmark is the paper's empirical centerpiece. Best-tier reviewers see the 'global hurts S0 by 5.6×' result as the most compelling instantiation of the bias identity — a low-error subgroup gets WORSE after correction, which is exactly the failure mode the paper identifies. The progression from n=50 (HB struggles) to n=100 (HB wins) to n=500 (converged) maps cleanly to the budget guidance in exp-006. Honest disclosure of n=50 boundary makes the n≥100 recommendation credible.\\n\\n\\nSemi-synthetic design validates the bias identity in a realistic annotation pipeline: GPT-4.1 on TweetEval sentiment produces genuine subgroup-varying misclassification (Δπ=0.20), not synthetic noise injection. Best-tier reviewers accept this because: (1) the DGP is fully specified (outcome generation + treatment assignment + CATE injection), satisfying the 'semi-synthetic transparency' requirement, (2) Δπ=0.20 matches the exp-012 pilot finding (Δπ=0.200 from GPT-4.1), confirming the heterogeneity is real, not assumed, (3) the +60% RMSE improvement at n=100/S3 demonstrates HB's value precisely where it matters most — the high-error minority subgroup. The n=50 degradation is disclosed honestly and positioned as a boundary condition, not a failure. NLP reviewers see 'real LLM + real corpus' as strong validation even though CATEs are injected. The 12% minority subgroup size (S2, S3) maps to realistic CSS scenarios (e.g., minority demographic groups in survey data).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Real-Data Case Study (Pattern Consistency)\",\n    \"description\": \"UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech corpus. N=2496 comments. GPT-4.1 annotation. Subgroups: platform_group × text_length (K=4). Pattern consistency analysis, NOT ground-truth CATE benchmark. Bootstrap CIs on point estimate differences.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"dataset\": \"UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\",\n      \"n_comments\": 2496,\n      \"delta_pi\": 0.117,\n      \"global_FPR\": 0.139,\n      \"global_FNR\": 0.533,\n      \"misclass_range\": \"0.266 - 0.383\",\n      \"echte_vs_global_avg_bias\": \"EC-HTE does not outperform global (high FNR limits correction)\",\n      \"key_pattern\": \"Δπ=0.117 confirms real-world heterogeneity exists\",\n      \"honest_limitation\": \"High FNR (53%) makes CM near-singular for positive class\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Real-Data Misclassification Heterogeneity (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Subgroup\",\n          \"n\",\n          \"FPR\",\n          \"FNR\",\n          \"Overall Misclass Rate\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"plat0_long\",\n            \"624\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.266\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"plat0_short\",\n            \"624\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.62\",\n            \"0.337\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"plat23_long\",\n            \"624\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"0.55\",\n            \"0.303\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"plat23_short\",\n            \"624\",\n            \"0.15\",\n            \"0.59\",\n            \"0.383\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Global (pooled)\",\n            \"2496\",\n            \"0.139\",\n            \"0.533\",\n            \"0.322\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A5: Real-Data CATE Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Avg |Bias|\",\n          \"Bootstrap 95% CI Width\",\n          \"Note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Naive\",\n            \"0.210\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"Baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Global Corrected\",\n            \"0.273\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"FNR=53% amplifies noise\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"EC-HTE\",\n            \"0.313\",\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"Wide CIs due to near-singular CM\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Note\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"No ground-truth CATE; pattern comparison only\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers (70-85% accept EC-HTE as useful) interpret this correctly: the negative real-data result is a feature, not a bug. It demonstrates the diagnostic criterion's value — a practitioner would compute Δπ=0.117 and FNR=0.533, conclude the classifier quality is insufficient for stratified correction, and stick with global correction or improve the classifier. This directly validates Claim 3 (diagnostic) by showing a real scenario where the diagnostic would correctly advise AGAINST EC-HTE. Reviewers note the honest framing approvingly: 'the authors show both success and failure cases.' Revision request: one reviewer asks for a second real dataset with lower FNR to show EC-HTE working — this is classified as minor revision.\\n\\n\\nThis is a negative/mixed result that STRENGTHENS the best-tier narrative. The real-data case shows: (1) misclassification heterogeneity exists in real LLM annotation (Δπ=0.117, statistically significant), validating the premise that global correction is mis-specified in practice, (2) EC-HTE does NOT outperform when FNR≈53% (near-random for positive class) — this connects to the honest boundary condition from exp-015/016 and the 'classifier quality threshold' limitation. Best-tier reviewers see this as mature scholarship: the authors don't cherry-pick favorable datasets, they show where the method works AND where it doesn't, and explain WHY (near-singular CM). The 'pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark' framing is accepted because reviewers agree no ground-truth CATE exists for observational data.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Diagnostic Criterion Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Compute Δπ × Δτ product from pilot data across all experimental settings. ROC analysis of threshold for predicting when global correction fails. Cross-validated (NOT in-sample) AUC.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"subgroup_level_auc\": 0.833,\n      \"subgroup_correlation\": 0.86,\n      \"setting_level_auc\": 0.49,\n      \"low_pi_hurt_pct\": 84.6,\n      \"high_pi_hurt_pct\": 15.4,\n      \"theory_alignment\": \"consistent with exp-002 formula\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Diagnostic Criterion Performance (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Level\",\n          \"AUC\",\n          \"Correlation\",\n          \"Precision@80%Recall\",\n          \"Note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Subgroup-level\",\n            \"0.833\",\n            \"0.86\",\n            \"0.79\",\n            \"Primary diagnostic\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Setting-level\",\n            \"0.49\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.51\",\n            \"Near random (expected)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A6: Diagnostic by Subgroup Δπ Quartile\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Δπ Quartile\",\n          \"Hurt Rate (%)\",\n          \"Mean Amplification\",\n          \"Diagnostic Recommendation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Q1 (lowest Δπ)\",\n            \"84.6%\",\n            \"2.8x\",\n            \"Flag: likely over-corrected\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Q2\",\n            \"62.3%\",\n            \"1.6x\",\n            \"Caution: mixed\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Q3\",\n            \"38.1%\",\n            \"0.9x\",\n            \"OK: near breakeven\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Q4 (highest Δπ)\",\n            \"15.4%\",\n            \"0.5x\",\n            \"Safe: under-corrected, global helps\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers (65-80% acceptance for diagnostic) see AUC=0.833 as 'good enough for practical use.' The key selling point is zero cost — the diagnostic requires only a pilot confusion matrix, which practitioners already compute. Even without adopting EC-HTE, knowing when global correction backfires has standalone value. The setting-level AUC=0.49 is a limitation that reviewers note but don't penalize heavily, because the subgroup-level diagnostic is what matters for the 'which subgroups to worry about' decision. One reviewer may request cross-validation details — this is a minor revision request.\\n\\n\\nSubgroup-level AUC=0.833 is strong for a zero-cost diagnostic. Best-tier reviewers accept this as 'useful practical tool' (65-80% acceptance range). The setting-level AUC=0.49 (near random) is an honest disclosure — the diagnostic works at the subgroup level (predicting WHICH subgroups get hurt) but not at the setting level (predicting WHETHER any subgroup gets hurt). This is theoretically consistent: the bias identity is a per-subgroup statement (each subgroup has its own Δπ_k × Δτ_k), not a global statement. Reviewers see this distinction as demonstrating understanding of the theory's scope. The 84.6% hurt rate for low-Δπ subgroups vs 15.4% for high-Δπ subgroups confirms the asymmetry predicted by the identity: low-error subgroups are over-corrected by global pooling. This is the 'debiasing hurts' mechanism in action.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Expert Label Budget Sensitivity\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic sweep: n_expert ∈ {50,100,250,500,1000,2000} × K ∈ {2,4} × regime ∈ {extreme,moderate}. Find RMSE crossover where EC-HTE beats global. 288 total configs, 100 MC runs each.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_configs\": 288,\n      \"runtime_min\": 110,\n      \"crossover_K2_extreme\": 250,\n      \"crossover_K2_moderate\": \"never (global always better on RMSE)\",\n      \"crossover_K4_extreme\": 1000,\n      \"crossover_K4_moderate\": 2000,\n      \"hb_beats_mle_pct\": \"87.5% (21/24 configs)\",\n      \"convergence_threshold\": \"n≥1000: HB ≈ MLE (<0.5%)\",\n      \"best_hb_improvement\": \"K=4/moderate/n=50/sgC: +42.4% RMSE over MLE\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Expert Budget Crossover Points (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"K\",\n          \"Regime\",\n          \"Crossover n_expert\",\n          \"HB RMSE at Crossover\",\n          \"Global RMSE at Crossover\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"Extreme\",\n            \"250\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.095\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"Moderate\",\n            \"Never\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4\",\n            \"Extreme\",\n            \"1000\",\n            \"0.068\",\n            \"0.072\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4\",\n            \"Moderate\",\n            \"2000\",\n            \"0.055\",\n            \"0.058\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A7: Full Budget Sweep\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"n_expert\",\n          \"K\",\n          \"Regime\",\n          \"HB RMSE\",\n          \"MLE RMSE\",\n          \"Global RMSE\",\n          \"HB Wins?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"50\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.228\",\n            \"0.248\",\n            \"0.105\",\n            \"vs MLE yes, vs Global no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"100\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.142\",\n            \"0.145\",\n            \"0.098\",\n            \"vs MLE yes, vs Global no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"250\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.093\",\n            \"0.095\",\n            \"Both\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"500\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.072\",\n            \"0.073\",\n            \"0.092\",\n            \"Both\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"50\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.310\",\n            \"0.382\",\n            \"0.128\",\n            \"vs MLE yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"250\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.135\",\n            \"0.140\",\n            \"0.110\",\n            \"vs MLE yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1000\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"extreme\",\n            \"0.068\",\n            \"0.069\",\n            \"0.072\",\n            \"Both\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers want actionable budget guidance. The 'Expert Budget Recommendation' subsection distills this: 'For K=2 with high heterogeneity, 250 expert labels suffice. For K=4, budget 1000. When diagnostic indicates moderate heterogeneity, global correction is the safer choice.' This connects Claims 2 and 3 into a coherent decision framework. The HB vs MLE comparison (87.5% HB wins) is a secondary but reassuring result — it justifies the Bayesian machinery over simpler alternatives.\\n\\n\\nBudget sensitivity is critical for Claim 2 acceptance (70-85% in best tier). The crossover points provide actionable guidance: K=2/extreme at n=250 is practical (typical CSS study can afford 250 expert labels), K=4/extreme at n=1000 is feasible but expensive. The 'K=2/moderate never crosses' result is an honest limitation that HELPS the narrative — it connects directly to Claim 3 (diagnostic): when heterogeneity is moderate, the diagnostic correctly advises against EC-HTE. Best-tier reviewers see this as 'the method knows when to use itself.' The 87.5% HB≥MLE rate confirms partial pooling rarely hurts (James-Stein guarantee in symmetric DGP). Convergence at n≥1000 shows HB advantage is most valuable in the data-scarce regime where it matters most.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"PPCI + DSL Baseline Comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Four DGPs: A (covariate-only error), B (outcome-only error), C (both errors), D (no error). 8 methods including EC-HTE, PPCI, EC-HTE+PPCI composition, DSL per-subgroup. 6912 total rows, 100 MC runs per config.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_rows\": 6912,\n      \"dgps\": [\n        \"A_covariate_only\",\n        \"B_outcome_only\",\n        \"C_both_errors\",\n        \"D_no_error\"\n      ],\n      \"methods\": 8,\n      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_ppci_bias\": 0.005,\n      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_bias\": 0.178,\n      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ppci_bias\": 0.004,\n      \"dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_naive_bias\": 0.251,\n      \"bias_reduction_composition_vs_echte\": \"35x\",\n      \"coverage_ec_hte_ppci\": 1.0,\n      \"dgp_a_ec_hte_ppci_equals_ec_hte\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Method Comparison Across Error Types (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"DGP\",\n          \"Method\",\n          \"|Bias| (z0)\",\n          \"RMSE (z0)\",\n          \"Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"A (cov only)\",\n            \"EC-HTE\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.089\",\n            \"0.94\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"A (cov only)\",\n            \"PPCI\",\n            \"0.045\",\n            \"0.132\",\n            \"0.88\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"A (cov only)\",\n            \"EC-HTE+PPCI\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.089\",\n            \"0.94\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"B (out only)\",\n            \"EC-HTE\",\n            \"0.085\",\n            \"0.112\",\n            \"0.87\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"B (out only)\",\n            \"PPCI\",\n            \"0.006\",\n            \"0.098\",\n            \"0.95\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"B (out only)\",\n            \"EC-HTE+PPCI\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.095\",\n            \"0.96\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"C (both)\",\n            \"Naive\",\n            \"0.251\",\n            \"0.269\",\n            \"0.72\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"C (both)\",\n            \"EC-HTE\",\n            \"0.178\",\n            \"0.226\",\n            \"0.83\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"C (both)\",\n            \"PPCI\",\n            \"0.004\",\n            \"0.261\",\n            \"0.91\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"C (both)\",\n            \"EC-HTE+PPCI\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.225\",\n            \"1.00\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"D (none)\",\n            \"All methods\",\n            \"~0.002\",\n            \"~0.045\",\n            \"~0.95\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A8: DSL Per-Subgroup Baseline\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Config\",\n          \"DSL |Bias|\",\n          \"EC-HTE |Bias|\",\n          \"DSL RMSE\",\n          \"EC-HTE RMSE\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme/n=250\",\n            \"0.015\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.115\",\n            \"0.089\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme/n=500\",\n            \"0.010\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.085\",\n            \"0.072\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme/n=500\",\n            \"0.022\",\n            \"0.012\",\n            \"0.142\",\n            \"0.120\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers see the composition result as elevating the contribution from 'yet another correction method' to 'principled framework for LLM annotation pipelines.' The EC-HTE+PPCI composition addresses the realistic scenario where both covariate and outcome errors coexist — which is the default in LLM annotation. The DSL comparison shows EC-HTE's structural advantage (mixing matrix exploitation + shrinkage) over generic per-subgroup correction. Reviewer concern about 'just use PPCI' is preempted by DGP A results: PPCI alone has higher variance when error is purely covariate-side.\\n\\n\\nThe PPCI composition result is the paper's strongest empirical finding for best tier. EC-HTE+PPCI achieves near-zero bias (0.005) in DGP C — a 35× improvement over EC-HTE alone. This proves 'necessary and sufficient in composition': EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, PPCI rectifier handles outcome error, composition handles both. Best-tier reviewers (NLP-oriented) see this as a complete pipeline solution: LLM annotation introduces BOTH error types, and the composition addresses both. The DGP A control (EC-HTE+PPCI = EC-HTE) confirms no double-correction — PPCI correctly contributes nothing when there's no outcome error. DGP D (no error) confirms all methods reduce to oracle. The 'pure covariate-error' control (DGP A) demonstrates EC-HTE's structural advantage: PPCI alone has higher variance because it treats covariate error as a generic black-box correction, while EC-HTE exploits the mixing matrix structure.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multiple LLM Annotator Comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Six OpenAI models: GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4-turbo, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4.1, GPT-5.4. TweetEval sentiment. Measure per-subgroup misclassification heterogeneity across models. Test structural persistence of heterogeneity.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"models_completed\": 6,\n      \"all_delta_pi_above_005\": true,\n      \"effective_delta_pi_range\": \"0.080 - 0.198\",\n      \"worst_subgroup_consensus\": \"True_medium (5/6 models)\",\n      \"spearman_significant_pairs\": 4,\n      \"gpt35_delta_pi\": 0.198,\n      \"gpt41_delta_pi\": 0.098,\n      \"gpt4o_delta_pi\": 0.08,\n      \"gpt5x_delta_pi\": 0.114\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A9: Cross-Model Misclassification Heterogeneity (Appendix)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Overall Accuracy\",\n          \"Effective Δπ\",\n          \"Worst Subgroup\",\n          \"Best Subgroup\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GPT-3.5-turbo\",\n            \"0.78\",\n            \"0.198\",\n            \"True_medium (0.39)\",\n            \"False_medium (0.10)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GPT-4-turbo\",\n            \"0.83\",\n            \"0.149\",\n            \"True_medium (0.28)\",\n            \"False_medium (0.08)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GPT-4o\",\n            \"0.85\",\n            \"0.080\",\n            \"True_medium (0.22)\",\n            \"False_medium (0.07)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GPT-4o-mini\",\n            \"0.82\",\n            \"0.136\",\n            \"True_medium (0.30)\",\n            \"False_medium (0.09)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GPT-4.1\",\n            \"0.86\",\n            \"0.098\",\n            \"True_medium (0.24)\",\n            \"False_short (0.07)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GPT-5.4\",\n            \"0.84\",\n            \"0.114\",\n            \"True_medium (0.26)\",\n            \"False_medium (0.08)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Moved to appendix per paper framing guidelines. But best-tier reviewers reference it in reviews: 'The multi-model analysis convincingly demonstrates that heterogeneity is structural.' The fact that even GPT-5.4 shows Δπ=0.114 preempts the 'future models will fix this' objection.\\n\\n\\nThis experiment validates the paper's core premise: misclassification heterogeneity is STRUCTURAL, not model-specific. All 6 models across 3 generations (GPT-3.5 through GPT-5.4) show Δπ > 0.05 — the threshold where the bias identity predicts meaningful amplification. Best-tier reviewers see this as strong evidence that the problem won't 'go away with better models.' The worst-subgroup consensus (True_medium, 5/6 models) suggests text features driving misclassification are stable across architectures. The Spearman correlations (4 significant pairs) show error patterns are correlated across models — the same subgroups are hard for all LLMs. This is a P1 experiment that moves to appendix but provides crucial evidence for the 'this matters in practice' argument. Best-tier reviewers cite this as supporting evidence when rating practical significance.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Adaptive Expert Annotation Allocation\",\n    \"description\": \"CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error subgroups. Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation. Stage 3 experiment — builds on exp-001 infrastructure and exp-006 budget analysis.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"allocation_strategies\": [\n        \"uniform\",\n        \"uncertainty_only\",\n        \"cate_weighted\"\n      ],\n      \"uniform_rmse_k4_n500\": 0.12,\n      \"uncertainty_rmse_k4_n500\": 0.105,\n      \"cate_weighted_rmse_k4_n500\": 0.095,\n      \"cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uniform\": \"~21%\",\n      \"cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uncertainty\": \"~10%\",\n      \"effective_crossover_reduction\": \"Reduces K=4 crossover from n=1000 to ~700\",\n      \"coverage_all_strategies\": \"0.92-0.95\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A10: Allocation Strategy Comparison (Appendix)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Strategy\",\n          \"K\",\n          \"n_expert\",\n          \"RMSE\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Crossover n\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Uniform\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"500\",\n            \"0.120\",\n            \"0.93\",\n            \"1000\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Uncertainty-only\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"500\",\n            \"0.105\",\n            \"0.93\",\n            \"850\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CATE-weighted\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"500\",\n            \"0.095\",\n            \"0.94\",\n            \"700\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Uniform\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"250\",\n            \"0.091\",\n            \"0.94\",\n            \"250\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CATE-weighted\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"250\",\n            \"0.082\",\n            \"0.94\",\n            \"180\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"P2 experiment positioned as 'practical extension.' Best-tier reviewers note it approvingly but don't consider it a core contribution. One reviewer may suggest this as future work rather than appendix material — minor framing decision, not a weakness.\\n\\n\\nP2 experiment — appendix material. Under best tier, reviewers see adaptive allocation as a nice-to-have extension that demonstrates the framework's practical flexibility. The 21% RMSE improvement over uniform allocation is meaningful but not dramatic — it confirms that allocation matters but doesn't change the paper's main conclusions. The 'reduces crossover from n=1000 to ~700' result tightens the budget guidance from exp-006 but is incremental. Best-tier reviewers mention this favorably in 'additional contributions' but don't weight it heavily in their overall assessment. The CATE-weighted strategy's advantage comes from concentrating expert labels where both error and effect heterogeneity are highest — this is a direct application of the Δπ×Δτ diagnostic (Claim 3) to allocation, creating a nice conceptual link.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Robustness to Misspecified Subgroup Structure\",\n    \"description\": \"Test EC-HTE when subgroups are noisy/overlapping. Noise levels: 0%, 10%, 25%, 50% subgroup misassignment. K={2,4}. Coverage as decisive metric.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"noise_levels\": [\n        0,\n        0.1,\n        0.25,\n        0.5\n      ],\n      \"total_runs\": 800,\n      \"k2_coverage_hb_range\": \"0.945 - 0.958\",\n      \"k4_coverage_hb_range\": \"0.946 - 0.960\",\n      \"k2_coverage_naive_range\": \"0.875 - 0.897\",\n      \"hb_beats_mle_all_configs\": true,\n      \"graceful_convergence_at_50pct\": true,\n      \"coverage_drop_0_to_50_noise\": \"-1.3pp (K=2), -1.0pp (K=4)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A11: Robustness to Subgroup Noise (Appendix)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Noise (%)\",\n          \"K\",\n          \"HB Coverage\",\n          \"Global Coverage\",\n          \"Naive Coverage\",\n          \"HB RMSE\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0%\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.958\",\n            \"0.925\",\n            \"0.875\",\n            \"0.089\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"10%\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.948\",\n            \"0.935\",\n            \"0.877\",\n            \"0.092\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"25%\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.950\",\n            \"0.932\",\n            \"0.890\",\n            \"0.094\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"50%\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.945\",\n            \"0.945\",\n            \"0.897\",\n            \"0.096\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0%\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.956\",\n            \"0.930\",\n            \"0.904\",\n            \"0.120\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"10%\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.958\",\n            \"0.953\",\n            \"0.916\",\n            \"0.122\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"25%\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.960\",\n            \"0.948\",\n            \"0.909\",\n            \"0.125\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"50%\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.946\",\n            \"0.944\",\n            \"0.919\",\n            \"0.128\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers use this to rebut their own concern about subgroup sensitivity. The 'automatic shrinkage to global at high noise' property is intellectually satisfying — it means EC-HTE is a strict generalization of global correction, not a fragile alternative. Coverage as the decisive metric (not RMSE) is the right framing: practitioners care about interval validity more than point estimate precision.\\n\\n\\nRobustness is essential for reviewer acceptance of Claim 2 (70-85% range). The key result is graceful degradation: EC-HTE coverage drops only ~1pp from 0% to 50% noise, while maintaining nominal coverage (>94.5%) at all noise levels. This directly addresses reviewer concern #4 ('how sensitive to subgroup definition?') with a quantitative answer: 'not very.' The mechanism is HB partial pooling — at 50% noise, subgroups are essentially random, and HB automatically shrinks toward global (its prior), recovering global correction's behavior. This is the James-Stein guarantee in action: 'never much worse than the shrinkage target.' Naive coverage (87-90%) is consistently below nominal, confirming that misclassification bias — not subgroup misspecification — is the dominant source of coverage failure. Best-tier reviewers see this robustness analysis as demonstrating methodological maturity.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Causal Forest Integration\",\n    \"description\": \"EC-HTE correction applied to CausalForestDML subgroup estimates. Compare naive_forest, global_forest, ec_hte_forest. K={2,4} × regime × n_expert. 144 total rows, 100 MC runs.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_mc\": 100,\n      \"n_configs\": 8,\n      \"total_rows\": 144,\n      \"mean_abs_bias_naive_forest\": 0.156,\n      \"mean_abs_bias_global_forest\": 0.107,\n      \"mean_abs_bias_ec_hte_forest\": 0.061,\n      \"mean_rmse_naive_forest\": 0.178,\n      \"mean_rmse_global_forest\": 0.166,\n      \"mean_rmse_ec_hte_forest\": 0.229,\n      \"debiasing_hurts_max_ratio\": \"5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\",\n      \"debiasing_hurts_cases\": 9,\n      \"k2_n500_ec_hte_bias_range\": \"0.004-0.013 (near zero)\",\n      \"pattern_consistent_with_dim\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: CausalForest Integration Results (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Mean |Bias|\",\n          \"Mean RMSE\",\n          \"Max Amplification\",\n          \"K=2/n=500 |Bias|\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Naive Forest\",\n            \"0.156\",\n            \"0.178\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"0.125\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Global Forest\",\n            \"0.107\",\n            \"0.166\",\n            \"5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\",\n            \"0.033-0.109\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"EC-HTE Forest\",\n            \"0.061\",\n            \"0.229\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"0.004-0.013\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Debiasing-Hurts Cases in CausalForest (Main Paper)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Config\",\n          \"Subgroup\",\n          \"Naive |Bias|\",\n          \"Global |Bias|\",\n          \"Amplification\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme\",\n            \"sg0 (lowest π)\",\n            \"0.018\",\n            \"0.106\",\n            \"5.9x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=4/extreme\",\n            \"sg1\",\n            \"0.045\",\n            \"0.078\",\n            \"1.7x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=2/extreme\",\n            \"sg0 (lowest π)\",\n            \"0.022\",\n            \"0.063\",\n            \"2.9x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K=2/moderate\",\n            \"sg0\",\n            \"0.015\",\n            \"0.031\",\n            \"2.1x\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A12: Bias-RMSE Tradeoff by Budget\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"n_expert\",\n          \"K\",\n          \"EC-HTE |Bias|\",\n          \"EC-HTE RMSE\",\n          \"Global |Bias|\",\n          \"Global RMSE\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"100\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.025\",\n            \"0.185\",\n            \"0.071\",\n            \"0.112\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"250\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.012\",\n            \"0.128\",\n            \"0.065\",\n            \"0.108\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"500\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.006\",\n            \"0.095\",\n            \"0.059\",\n            \"0.105\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"250\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.032\",\n            \"0.265\",\n            \"0.095\",\n            \"0.145\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"500\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.018\",\n            \"0.195\",\n            \"0.088\",\n            \"0.140\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Best-tier reviewers see CausalForest results as the strongest evidence for practical relevance. The 5.9× amplification headline number comes from this experiment and is the paper's most eye-catching finding. The RMSE tradeoff is disclosed in a dedicated 'Bias-RMSE Tradeoff' subsection — reviewers appreciate the transparency and note that at n≥500, the tradeoff largely resolves. The pattern consistency between DIM and CausalForest ('same direction, amplified magnitude') strengthens the theoretical claim that the bias identity captures a fundamental mechanism, not an estimator-specific artifact. Combined with exp-017 (R-learner replication), this establishes estimator-agnosticism as a validated property.\\n\\nCausalForest integration is P0 because Claim 2 explicitly mentions CausalForestDML — without this experiment, there's a claim-evidence gap that reviewers would flag as a killshot. The results show two key patterns: (1) EC-HTE achieves 61% bias reduction vs naive and 43% vs global, confirming the correction generalizes beyond DIM to nonlinear CATE estimators, (2) the 'debiasing hurts' effect is AMPLIFIED with CausalForest (5.9× for K=4/extreme vs 2.41× for DIM) — nonlinear estimation compounds the misclassification-induced bias. This amplification is the headline '5.9×' number in the abstract. Best-tier reviewers accept the RMSE tradeoff (EC-HTE RMSE 0.229 > global 0.166) because it's disclosed honestly and explained: variance-bias tradeoff from per-subgroup CM estimation. At n=500/K=2, EC-HTE bias is near-zero (0.004-0.013) with competitive RMSE, showing the tradeoff resolves with sufficient expert labels. The '5.9× amplification with CausalForest' result is particularly impactful for NLP audience — many CSS researchers use causal forests, so this directly threatens their existing workflows.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1297,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1297}}}
